<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xerxes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/486-bce">486</a>–465 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Xerxes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xerxes I was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from 486 to 465 BCE, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. He led the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> in 480 BCE, which won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> but was defeated at Salamis and, in 479 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. Accounts of the Achaemenid Empire discuss him in the context of the empire's military history and the limits of its westward expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darafsh · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xerxes was born around 518 BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atossa">Atossa</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>. Greek sources describe the upbringing of Persian princes: raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, taught to ride and hunt from the age of seven, and given four aristocratic teachers from fourteen. It is unknown whether he learned to read or write. From 498 BCE he lived in the royal palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><p>When Darius prepared to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 487–486 BCE, Persian law required him to name a successor, and he chose Xerxes, his eldest son by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atossa">Atossa</a>, over Artobazan, who was born before Darius became king. Xerxes was crowned between October and December 486 BCE, at about thirty-two, and no one at court or among the subject nations is recorded as challenging him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Xerxes put down a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in January 484 BCE and made his full brother Achaemenes satrap there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> rebelled at least twice: under Bel-shimanni in 484 BCE, a revolt that lasted about two weeks, and under Shamash-eriba from 482 BCE, defeated in March 481 BCE after a long siege. Afterwards Xerxes dropped the title King of Babylon and divided the large Babylonian satrapy into smaller units. Classical authors claim he wrecked the temples and removed the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, which the modern historian Amélie Kuhrt considers unlikely.</p><p>From 483 BCE he prepared the invasion of Greece, with a canal cut through the Athos peninsula and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridges</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>. In 480 BCE he marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and Artemisium he took and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, then lost the sea battle at Salamis. He withdrew with most of his army, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>, who was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> the next year. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> he finished works begun by Darius at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, including the Gate of All Nations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apadana">Apadana</a>, and built a palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 465 BCE Artabanus, commander of the royal guard, killed Xerxes with the help of the eunuch Aspamitres. Ctesias says Artabanus then blamed the crown prince Darius, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> says he killed Darius first. Xerxes's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a> took the throne. Xerxes held to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> as supreme deity, though scholars do not agree whether he was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>.</p><p>Greek and Roman sources present him largely in a negative light. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>'s play The Persians (472 BCE) shows a hubristic ruler, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives a fuller portrait that Richard Stoneman reads as nuanced and tragic, while Pierre Briant calls it biased. He is identified with Ahasuerus in the Book of Esther, and is the hero of Handel's opera Serse (1738).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xerxes I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its campaigns included Cyrus's conquest of Lydia, where camels were reportedly deployed to unsettle the Lydian cavalry, the conquest of Egypt (525 BCE), Darius's Scythian campaign (c. 513 BCE), and <b>Xerxes</b>'s massive invasion of Greece (480 BCE), which brought victory at Thermopylae and the sack of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the urging of Themistocles the city then built a fleet of some 200 triremes, which formed the core of the Greek navy that defeated <b>Xerxes I</b> at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, and Athenians fought at Plataea in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ionian Revolt and two Persian invasions of Greece, the Allies defeated the forces of <b>Xerxes I</b> at the Battle of Plataea and the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE, then took Sestos and, in 478 BCE, Byzantion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Darius I most of the people of Barca in Cyrenaica were deported there, and under <b>Xerxes I</b> the descendants of Greek priests from near Didyma were forcibly relocated to the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Macedonia was never made a satrapy, but it supplied troops: Alexander I supported <b>Xerxes I</b> in 480–479 BCE, and Macedonians fought on the Persian side at the Battle of Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 506 BCE the Thebans repelled an inroad into Attica; in 480 BCE they sent 400 men to Thermopylae, who were defeated alongside the Spartans, and then fought for <b>Xerxes I</b> at the Battle of Plataea in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30,52&amp;year=-476" aria-label="Persis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Xerxes I"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4409 4979h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4409 4979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30,52&amp;year=-476">What was here in 476 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/darius-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Darius I</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; r. 522–486 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/atossa"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Atossa</span><span class="tp">Mother &#183; c. 550–475 BCE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/artaxerxes-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Artaxerxes I</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; d. 424 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…a sudden pity, when I thought of the shortness of man's life, and considered that of all this host, so numerous as it is, not one will be alive when a hundred years are gone by.&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 7 (reviewing his army at Abydos) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account; he wept, and his uncle Artabanus asked him why &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_7" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for great empires can only be conquered by great risks.&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 7 (to Artabanus, before crossing into Europe) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_7" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My men have behaved like women, my women like men!&#8221;</p><footer>Herodotus, Histories, book 8 (watching the battle of Salamis) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account, on seeing the ship of Queen Artemisia in the battle &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_8" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Far better is it to have a stout heart always, and suffer one's share of evils, than to be ever fearing what may happen, and never incur a mischance.&#8221;</p><footer>To his uncle Artabanus at Abydos, 480 BCE (Herodotus, Histories, book 7) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_7" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Success for the most part attends those who act boldly, not those who weigh everything, and are slack to venture.&#8221;</p><footer>To his uncle Artabanus at Abydos, 480 BCE (Herodotus, Histories, book 7) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_7" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
